WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.080
The brain's cleansing system has
only been recently discovered.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:07.200
It may be a key component
to healthy cognition.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.280
In this video and the next,

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.476
we look at how the
glymphatic system works and,

00:00:11.476 --> 00:00:12.300
more importantly,

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.301
what we can do to
make it work even better.

00:00:15.301 --> 00:00:22.920
"How Much Sleep Is Needed for
Glymphatic Flow (Brain Cleaning)?"

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:26.520
Sleep is a great mystery.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.800
A trait shared
across animal species,

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.840
sleep must be
of vital importance

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.880
to survive
natural selection pressures

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.160
to eliminate
such a vulnerable state.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.600
Indeed, cringeworthy experiments
have shown

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.160
that keeping animals awake
long enough

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:44.640
can be fatal within
eleven to thirty-two days.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.600
It turns out
sleep is of-the-brain,

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.120
by the brain,
and for the brain.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:54.300
One function of sleep that has
been elucidated in recent years

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.720
is the clearance
of toxic waste byproducts

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:01.200
through a newly discovered
drainage system in the brain.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.800
With the invention
of the encephalogram, EEG,

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.780
to measure brain wave activity,

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:09.300
the scientific world
was quickly disabused

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.240
of the notion that sleep was
a time of rest for the brain.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.040
During certain stages of sleep

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.380
there is brain-wide activity
going on,

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:19.620
but what was
the brain actively doing?

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.320
More than 2,000 years ago,

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.520
Aristotle proposed that sleep
helps the body clean the blood.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.840
Today, we know sleep may help
the body clean the brain.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:35.580
Until 2012, we thought
that the brain was singular

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:39.000
among organs for recycling
nearly all of its own waste.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.040
It had to,
since it was separated

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.980
from the rest of the body
by the blood-brain barrier.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:47.640
But the barrier that keeps
toxins out of the brain

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.100
presumably keeps toxins in.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.380
Then, in 2012,

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.560
a brain-wide
fluid transport network

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:59.280
was discovered,
termed the glymphatic system.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.760
By microscopically tracking dye

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.140
injected into the brains
of mice,

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:06.840
scientists discovered
fluid-filled tunnels

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.640
surrounding blood vessels
in the brain.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.400
The pressure wave
of arterial pulses

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:13.800
with every heartbeat
milks the fluid along

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.920
before eventually draining
into the cerebrospinal fluid

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:17.820
surrounding the brain.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:20.940
What does this
have to do with sleep?

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:25.380
The whole system is only
really active when sleeping.

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:28.020
During wakefulness,
these tunnels are clamped down,

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.540
reducing glymphatic flow
by 90 percent.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:34.260
The thought is the fluid shifts
might interfere

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:37.080
with targeted neurotransmitter
chemical communication

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:38.340
in the awake state.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:42.660
So the biological need for sleep
may reflect the need

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:45.360
for the brain to enter
into a state to filter out

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.600
potentially neurotoxic waste
products like beta amyloid,

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.700
which is implicated
in Alzheimer's disease.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.400
Perhaps this could help explain

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:56.400
why those who routinely get
fewer than seven hours of sleep

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:57.900
a night are at increased risk

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.600
of developing cognitive
disorders such as dementia.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:04.500
Randomizing individuals
to have their sleep disrupted

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:06.060
by a series of beeps

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.400
administered through headphones
in a sleep lab

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:12.480
increases amyloid levels,
whereas improving sleep,

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:16.140
by treating sleep apnea patients
with CPAP, for example,

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:18.300
improves slow wave activity,

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:21.480
deep sleep, and appears
to lower amyloid levels.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:24.660
PET scans show
even a single all-nighter

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:26.160
can cause
a significant increase

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:29.520
in accumulation of beta amyloid
in critical brain areas.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:34.440
The problem is that
glymphatic brain filtration

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:36.720
appears to decline with aging.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.780
Old mice only have
10 to 20 percent

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:42.360
the glymphatic function
of young mice.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:44.220
This could be due
to a number of factors.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:45.180
As we age,

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:48.300
we experience less
of the deep, slow wave sleep,

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:51.000
the type of sleep during
which brain waste clearance

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:52.380
appears to be the most active.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.680
Further contributing
to the stagnancy,

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.800
our arteries tend
to stiffen as we age,

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:02.100
reducing the pulsations
that drive the glymphatic pump.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:05.280
That also offers
one potential explanation

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:07.920
as to why hypertension
is tied to dementia.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:10.560
The thickening of artery walls
with high blood pressure

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.300
also has a stiffening effect.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:16.800
How can we counter this
age-related glymphatic decline

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.600
and keep our brains cleaner?

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:22.680
We'll explore
just that question, next.

